    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Joints are not evenly distributed within some geologic folds, but rather are localized in narrow zones. The presence of bedding plane faults within a\r\nflexure may play an important role in this localization of fractures. Flexural slip is common in folds because they are comprised of a heterogeneous stack of rock strata. Fractures are likely to be associated spatially with regions of greater curvature and with larger slip gradients on bedding plane faults. Numerical models of the folding process, including inelastic frictional deformation along bedding planes have been developed using the boundary element method (BEM). This will help geologists interpret potential fracture clustering mechanisms and lead to the development of predictive methods for sub-surface fracture distributions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
